data.content:
# .NET 8 Migration: BackgroundProcessor

## Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET Core+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` also removed** — no equivalent needed
3. **`ThreadAbortException` is never raised** by the runtime

## Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _workerTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — clean up partial work
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit loop; no ResetAbort equivalent needed
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _cts.Cancel();

        if (_workerTask is not null)
        {
            // Wait up to timeout, then give up (but task is already
            // cancelled — it won't linger if GetNextItemAsync honors the token)
            var effectiveTimeout = timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(2);
            await _workerTask.WaitAsync(effectiveTimeout)
                             .ConfigureAwait(ConfigureAwaitOptions.SuppressThrowing);
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();
        _workerTask?.Wait(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
        _cts.Dispose();
    }
}
```

## Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **Blocking `GetNextItem()`** | If it ignores the token, cancellation hangs forever. Always pass `CancellationToken` to blocking/async calls. |
| **`Thread.Abort` → no replacement** | There is no forced-kill equivalent. You *must* make your work cooperatively cancellable. |
| **`volatile bool` flag** | Works but can't interrupt a blocked call. `CancellationToken` can. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` in Stop** | Replace with `await Task.WaitAsync(timeout)` — non-blocking and precise. |
| **`catch (ThreadAbortException)`** | Change to `catch (OperationCanceledException)`. Add a `when` guard so you don't swallow unrelated cancellations. |
| **`ResetAbort()`** | No equivalent needed — `OperationCanceledException` is a normal exception that doesn't auto-rethrow. |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Store the `Task` and await it during shutdown or you'll miss unobserved exceptions. |

## If You Can't Make Everything Async

For sync-only code that blocks (e.g., legacy I/O):

```csharp
// Wrap the blocking call so cancellation still works
private WorkItem GetNextItem(CancellationToken token)
{
    // Poll with cancellation instead of blocking indefinitely
    while (!_queue.TryDequeue(out var item))
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
        Thread.Sleep(50); // brief spin-wait
    }
    return item;
}
```

## Alternative: Use `BackgroundService`

For hosted apps (ASP.NET Core, Worker Services), prefer the built-in base class:

```csharp
public class MyProcessor : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            var item = await GetNextItemAsync(stoppingToken);
            await ProcessItemAsync(item, stoppingToken);
        }
    }
}

// Registration in Program.cs
builder.Services.AddHostedService<MyProcessor>();
```

This gives you **free lifecycle management** — the host handles startup, graceful shutdown, and timeout for you.
